---
title: Pharos
summary: A documentation-site kit on top of collections. Weave collections into a navigable site — nav tree, prev/next, search, sitemap, a shell — with the brains browser-safe and the corpus server-only.
related:
  - pharos/outline
  - pharos/shell
  - content/collections
---

A [collection](/docs/content/collections) answers **what exists** — a set of typed entries. Pharos answers **how they read as a site**: the nav tree, the order, the prev/next, the search index, the sitemap, the shell around the page. `ogygia/pharos` is a documentation-site kit built directly on the content pillar. **This site is one** — every page you are reading is woven, navigated, and searched by pharos.

You hand it collections and an arrangement; it mints the *brains* — small, data-returning, browser-safe functions the routes and the shell consume. The corpus itself never crosses the wire.

> **When to use.** Reach for pharos when a collection needs to become a *site*: a sidebar, "keep reading", a search box, `llms.txt`, versioned or localized docs. If you only need a list and a body on one page, `content()` alone is enough — pharos is the layer that turns a corpus into something you navigate.

## The whole thing in one file

`pharos()` takes an **outline** — how your collections arrange — and returns a `Site`. It lives in a **`.server.ts` module**, next to the collections it weaves:

```ts
// src/lib/site.server.ts — server-only: the corpus can never reach a client bundle
import { pharos, links } from 'ogygia/pharos';
import { docs } from './collections.server';

export const site = pharos({
  outline: docs,          // a bare collection weaves itself by convention (see below)
  prevNext: 'graph',      // "keep reading" follows the content graph
  checks: [links()]       // a broken in-prose link fails the build
});
```

That is the entire configuration for this site. `docs` is a `folder()` collection, so its `NN-` filename prefixes already encode order and its `+meta.json` sidecars name the sections — pharos reads that structure as **data** and weaves the tree with nothing more to say. Point pharos at several collections, or a `pick()` selection, or explicit groups when you want to arrange by hand — that is the [outline](/docs/pharos/outline).

> **Collections belong in a server module.** A collection's loader is an *eager glob* — the whole compiled corpus. Define it in a `.server.ts` / `.remote.ts` file (or under `src/lib/server/`) and Kit mechanically prevents any client module from importing it, so the corpus can never leak into a browser bundle. Define one elsewhere and ogygia **warns** you at build time. See [Content collections](/docs/content/collections).

## Brains, not a framework

`site` is a bag of read functions. Each returns plain data; none pulls the corpus into the browser:

| brain | returns | used by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `site.nav()` | the sidebar tree, hrefs resolved | the shell / a nav remote |
| `site.doc(slug)` | one page's view — data, headings, body, prev/next | the page component |
| `site.search(q)` | ranked hits | a search remote / the worker |
| `site.emit.*` | GET handlers — sitemap, llms.txt, RSS, raw markdown | `+server.ts` routes |
| `site.check()` | link-audit findings, as data | vitest / CI |
| `site.entries()` | every leaf slug | prerender `entries` |

Because they are data, **where** they run is your call. Prerender the nav, or fetch it per request; query search on the server, or ship the static index to a client worker. The [remotes](/docs/pharos/remotes) helper mints the common wire surface for you.

## The wire layer

The collection holds the compiled corpus — potentially megabytes of markdown. Pharos never serializes it. A page's **body** crosses the wire as a [held region](/docs/regions/held-regions) ticket (baked HTML, not source); everything else is metadata. The [`remotes()`](/docs/pharos/remotes) helper mints the common wire surface from `site` in a `.remote.ts` — server-only, so the corpus stays server-side and the browser gets only the ticketed data:

```ts
// src/lib/site.remote.ts — the wire layer
import { remotes } from 'ogygia/pharos/server';
import { site } from './site.server';

export const { nav, doc, search } = remotes(site, { base: '/docs' });
```

## Render it

The page component reads one brain and renders the view. The [`<Doc>`](/docs/pharos/shell) component lays out the article, the on-this-page rail, and prev/next from a `site.doc()` view; the [shell](/docs/pharos/shell) wraps it with the sidebar, header, and search:

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/docs/[...slug]/+page.svelte -->
<script>
  import { page } from '$app/state';
  import { Doc } from 'ogygia/pharos';
  import { doc } from '$lib/site.remote';

  const view = await doc(page.params.slug);
</script>

<Doc {view} />
```

## The map

Pharos is one kit with a few clear parts. Each has its own page:

- **[Outline](/docs/pharos/outline)** — weave collections into a tree. The spec grammar, filename conventions, the address map, prev/next.
- **[Dimensions](/docs/pharos/dimensions)** — one site across a matrix of versions or locales, with a switcher and graceful fallback.
- **[Shell & components](/docs/pharos/shell)** — `Frame` (headless) and `Shell` (batteries-included), plus `Doc`, `Sidebar`, `Pager`, and the theme.
- **[Remotes](/docs/pharos/remotes)** — the wire layer: `nav`, `doc`, `search`, prerendered or live.
- **[Search](/docs/pharos/search)** — the search brain, the static index, the on-device worker, the no-JS fallback.
- **[Emissions](/docs/pharos/emit)** — machine-facing serializations: sitemap, `llms.txt`, RSS, raw markdown.
- **[Checks](/docs/pharos/checks)** — the link audit and custom checks that fail the build, not the visitor.

## Do / don't

- **Do** keep `pharos()` and its collections in a browser-safe module, and mint the wire in a `.remote.ts` — that split is what keeps the corpus out of client bundles.
- **Do** let filename conventions carry structure (`NN-` order, `+meta.json` labels). Pharos reads them as data; you write no arrangement config for the common case.
- **Don't** pass a collection's bodies over the wire yourself. Let `site.doc()` bake the body into a region ticket — that is the only representation meant to cross.
- **Don't** reach for pharos to render a single standalone page. That is `content()` plus `<Region>`; pharos is the *site* around many.
